question:
Your team is preparing a quick CPI trend check for an operations briefing. Build a reusable PL/SQL procedure that, for a given CPI item and area, prints the most recent periods available (up to a fixed small limit) along with the CPI value and the month-over-month change. Because the CPI VALUE column is stored as text, you must defensively convert it to a number and keep going even if some rows contain non-numeric values.

Use a cursor to walk the most recent rows and compute changes in PL/SQL (not with analytic LAG). Add simple data-quality messages when you skip a bad row, and print a final count of printed rows. Make sure the procedure never throws an unhandled exception.

Requirements:
Procedure Name: cpi_trend_report
Parameters:
p_area_name IN BLS.CPI_U.area_name%TYPE
p_item_name IN BLS.CPI_U.item_name%TYPE
Output:
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('CPI Trend Report');
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Area=' || NVL(p_area_name, 'NULL') || ', Item=' || NVL(p_item_name, 'NULL'));
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('No rows matched the given area/item filters.');
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Period=' || TO_CHAR(rec.c_date, 'YYYY-MM') || ', Value=' || TO_CHAR(lv_curr_value) || ', MoM_Change=' || TO_CHAR(lv_mom_change));
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Skipping row due to non-numeric CPI value.');
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Printed rows=' || TO_CHAR(lv_printed_rows));
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('No data found.');
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Multiple rows returned.');
DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE('Unexpected error.');
Exception Handling:
WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN: 'No data found.'
WHEN TOO_MANY_ROWS THEN: 'Multiple rows returned.'
WHEN OTHERS THEN: 'Unexpected error.'

